{
  "_comment": [
    "Who wrote it, and when they died (replan §2.2, docs/03 §1 rule 1).",
    "",
    "PDMX's `composer_name` column is free text typed by whoever uploaded the file, and it is",
    "`NA` for 22,111 of the 36,150 deduplicated solo-piano rows. This table is what turns the",
    "rest into a decision: a matched name gives a death year, and licensing.composition_verdict",
    "turns that into `pd` or `in-copyright` against the 1930 bar.",
    "",
    "IMPORTANT — this is a *label*, not a gate (docs/00 D23). The owner's amendment of",
    "2026-09-06 says a personal build may carry anything the dataset marks public domain, so",
    "select.py records `compositionStatus` on every candidate and rejects nothing for it.",
    "`--strict-license` is where the label bites: the public Pages deploy refuses anything that",
    "is not `pd`, which is why the decoys below exist and are tested.",
    "",
    "`died` is the death year. `born` is carried because a composer string often has bracketed",
    "years in it (\"J.S. Bach (1685-1750)\") and select.py checks them against this table rather",
    "than believing them — an uploader who types the wrong years is telling you something.",
    "",
    "`aliases` are matched after folding: NFKD-normalised, lower-cased, punctuation and the",
    "words \"composed by\" / \"arr.\" / \"arranged by\" stripped, bracketed years removed. So",
    "\"J.S. Bach\" and \"js bach\" are one alias, and only spellings that fold differently need a",
    "row of their own.",
    "",
    "Growing it is P14's job: select.py prints the 200 most frequent unmatched strings, and a",
    "real composer among them is a line here — with the death year, whichever side of 1930 it",
    "falls on."
  ],
